|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/- Certainly! Below is a Python script that calculates the derivative using the Average Rate of Change Formula \( A(x) = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a} \). The user will input the values of \( a \) and \( b \) (which represent the points \( x_1 \) and \( x_2 \) respectively), and the function \( f(x) \) will be defined by the user. 

```python
def average_rate_of_change(a, b, f):
    # Calculate the function values at points a and b
    f_a = f(a)
    f_b = f(b)
    
    # Calculate the derivative using the average rate of change formula
    derivative = (f_b - f_a) / (b - a)
    
    return derivative

def main():
    # Function definition from user input
    def f(x):
        # Example function: f(x) = x^2
        return x ** 2
    
    # User input for points a and b
    a = float(input("Enter the value of a: "))
    b = float(input("Enter the value of b: "))
    
    # Calculate and print the derivative
    derivative = average_rate_of_change(a, b, f)
    print(f"The derivative at x = {a} and x = {b} is: {derivative}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

To use this script, you need to define the function \( f(x) \) that you want to differentiate. In the example provided, \( f(x) = x^2 \). You can replace this with any other function. When you run the script, it will prompt you to enter the values of \( a \) and \( b \), and it will output the derivative at those points.

